<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/advanced/painting-mediums">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A painting medium is <strong>a binder with a personality</strong>: pigment is the colour,
    but the binder — gum arabic, acrylic polymer, linseed oil, egg yolk, wax — decides how
    the colour dries, how it can be reworked, how light enters it, and how long it survives.
    The same ultramarine is luminous and literal in watercolour, flat and graphic in gouache,
    deep and jewel-like in oil glaze. The syllabus's AO2 strand — selecting media for their
    <em>inherent properties</em>, experimenting with them, and refining their use to express
    ideas — is exactly the knowledge this chapter organises: each medium's chemistry, its
    workflow consequences, and the craft tests that let you take creative risks with
    informed hands. The medium is not the message, but it is the physics of the message.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="watercolour, acrylic, oil, the others" widget="equationStep" config={paintCfg} />

  <h2>Watercolour: engineering with light and water</h2>
  <p>
    Watercolour is pigment and gum arabic in water, absorbed into sized paper. Its signature
    quality is <strong>transparency plus the paper as light source</strong>: light travels
    through the thin pigment film, scatters off the white paper, and returns filtered — the
    painting is literally backlit, which is why watercolour whites are <em>preserved paper</em>
    rather than applied paint (opaque white kills the glow) and why the discipline is
    planning: the artist paints the light by leaving it. The craft is water management on
    three settings. <strong>Wet-on-wet</strong>: pigment dropped into a flooded surface
    spreads by capillary flow into blooms and gradients — beautiful and uncontrollable, the
    medium's signature weather. <strong>Wet-on-dry</strong>: pigment on dry paper stops at
    the brush edge — the crisp shapes of botanical and architectural work.
    <strong>Glazing</strong>: transparent layers stacked after drying multiply like coloured
    glass — three magenta glazes each transmitting roughly two-thirds of the light leave
    about (2/3)³ ≈ 30% transmission: a deep, still-luminous crimson impossible to mix
    on the palette, because the stack <em>subtracts</em> light where mixing powders adds
    scattering. The unforgivingness is structural: watercolour re-wets, so every new layer
    threatens to lift the old — corrections are removal (lifting with a damp brush), not
    overpainting, and hesitation shows. The medium teaches commitment and value planning;
    that pedagogy is a large part of why it stays foundational in every art school.
  </p>

  <h2>Acrylic: polymer chemistry on fast-forward</h2>
  <p>
    Acrylic is pigment in a water-borne acrylic polymer <strong>emulsion</strong>: droplets
    of plastic suspended in water. Drying is coalescence — the water evaporates, droplets
    fuse into a continuous flexible film — which takes minutes and is then
    <strong>permanently waterproof</strong>. The workflow consequences are total: layer
    light over dark freely, correct endlessly (it overpaints everything including itself),
    and mix it with gels and pastes to control viscosity from ink to sculptural impasto —
    acrylic is the medium of <em>controlled surface</em>. Its costs are the mirror of its
    virtues: the open blending window is minutes (retarders and stay-wet palettes extend
    it; nothing extends it to oil's scale), colours darken slightly as the milky emulsion
    clears, and the dried film can read slightly plastic where oil's depth of tone reads
    organic. Acrylic also made <em>scale</em> cheap: the stain-and-flow school (Louis,
    Frankenthaler) and vast colour-field canvases exist because a water-thin, fast,
    cheaply permanent paint arrived in the 1950s — a medium change producing an art-historical
    change, AO2's lesson in miniature.
  </p>

  <h2>Oil: the slow chemistry of depth</h2>
  <p>
    Oil paint dries by neither evaporation nor cooling: linseed oil
    <strong>polymerises</strong> — oxygen cross-links its fatty acids into a solid
    translucent network over days to months, depending on pigment (earth colours and lead
    driers speed it; whites and blacks slow it). That clock is the medium's gift:
    <strong>days of open time</strong> mean blending, scraping, and adjusting wet-into-wet
    (alla prima — Sargent's single-sitting bravura), and the dried film accepts
    <strong>glazes</strong> — thin transparent colour over dry underpainting — the indirect
    method that built the old masters' flesh tones: light penetrates the glaze, reflects
    from the underlayer, and returns twice-filtered. Depth in oil painting is optical
    architecture. The one inviolable rule is <strong>fat over lean</strong>: each layer
    should contain slightly more oil (be "fatter") than the one below, because fat layers
    dry slower; a fat layer under a lean one means the top film sets and shrinks while the
    lower film still moves — differential stress, and the painting cracks (craquelure,
    the conservator's map of a workshop's impatience). The rule of thumb is adding a
    little more medium with each successive layer; the deeper chemistry is why paintings
    on correctly built grounds have survived five centuries while bad structure has not.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> oil is "stronger" than watercolour and therefore easier.
    It is easier to <em>correct</em> and harder to <em>judge</em>: colours shift as they
    dry (oil darkens slightly; glazes read wetter than they will dry), values deepen over
    months, and mistakes made in layer structure surface decades later. Watercolour's
    difficulty is instantaneous; oil's is deferred. Neither is the easy one — they fail on
    different clocks.
  </p>

  <h2>Gouache, tempera, encaustic, digital</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Gouache</strong> is watercolour made opaque: higher pigment load with white
    (or densely packed coarse particles) and often chalk, giving flat, matte, absolutely
    even colour that re-wets and rewelds — the illustrator's and designer's medium
    (consistent flats, photographable without glare), and the quick-sketch field medium
    of landscape painters. <strong>Egg tempera</strong> — pigment in egg yolk, the panel
    tradition of the 13th–15th centuries — dries in seconds to a hard, water-resistant
    film: it cannot blend, so form is built from fine <strong>hatched strokes</strong>
    layered over gesso (Botticelli, and every icon), achieving a crisp luminosity no
    other medium matches; the discipline is patience at the speed of watercolour with
    permanence approaching oil. <strong>Encaustic</strong> (pigmented beeswax, fused with
    heat) gives translucent layered colour you can scrape back into — the Fayum portraits'
    two-millennium survival is its credentials. <strong>Digital</strong> is every
    medium's properties simulated and one property — undo — added without limit. That
    unlimited undo is both the great accelerator ( fearless experimentation at zero
    material cost) and the great pedagogical risk: friction teaches judgement, and
    students who never commit never develop the commitment muscles. The AO2 position is
    deliberate: use digital's freedom to explore, and discipline it with media-specific
    constraints (limited layers, no undo for value decisions) when the goal is
    transferable skill.
  </p>

  <h2>Experiment and refinement: the AO2 working loop</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pigment chemistry is permanence:</strong> lightfastness ratings (ASTM I–II) — the alizarin crimson that fades and the ultramarine that never does; the conservation lesson's chemistry, at the purchasing stage.</li>
    <li><strong>Grounds matter:</strong> gesso (absorbent, for water media), oil grounds (sized, sealed) — paintings fail from the bottom up; the substrate is half the work (the architecture lesson's foundations, at tabletop scale).</li>
    <li><strong>Direct vs layered painting:</strong> alla prima (wet-into-wet, one sitting — Sargent's bravura) vs indirect (underpainting, glazes — the old masters' luminosity, the physics of light passing through layers); two complete aesthetics from the same oil paint.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety is part of craft:</strong> cadmiums and solvents demand ventilation and disposal discipline — the toxicology lesson's rules, studio edition.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The assessed behaviours: <strong>test boards</strong> (swatch every new pigment's
    tinting strength, opacity, and drying behaviour before it touches the work);
    <strong>colour charts and mixing trials</strong> that reveal each pigment's
    <em>bias</em> (ultramarine leans violet, phthalo leans green — knowing which blue
    makes which green is the difference between muddy and luminous mixes);
    <strong>recorded process</strong> (notes on dilution, timing, and grounds) that makes
    refinements repeatable; and then <strong>deliberate risk</strong> in the final piece —
    the syllabus rewards creative risk taken <em>on the basis of</em> tested technique,
    not in place of it. Restriction is itself a medium experiment: a two-colour palette,
    a monochrome study, or one brush for a whole painting frequently unlocks more
    development than adding materials, because limitation forces decisions the surplus
    of options defers.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The physics of opacity and depth.</strong> Opacity is a refractive-index
    argument: light scatters at every mismatch between pigment and surrounding medium.
    Titanium white (RI ≈ 2.7) in linseed oil (≈ 1.48) scatters ferociously — maximum
    hiding power; a pigment whose index sits close to its binder reads glassy. This
    explains the medium families from the inside: watercolour particles sit in air and
    water gaps on the paper (huge effective mismatch at their surfaces, but thin film and
    white backing — transparent filters); oil surrounds pigment in a closer-index medium,
    letting light penetrate deeper before scattering — the optical cause of oil's
    saturation and of the slow darkening as oil's index shifts with age; and load
    (pigment-volume concentration) tunes the same physics continuously, which is why a
    single tube of paint can be sheered from glaze to body colour.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Permanence, quantified.</strong> Lightfastness is measured, not felt: ASTM
    ratings (I = excellent, through V) come from accelerated exposure against Blue Wool
    standards; alizarin crimson (PR83, lake on alumina) fades measurably within decades —
    a reason modern quinacridones (PV19, lightfast I) displaced it — while phthalocyanines
    and iron oxides are effectively immortal. The fugitive-pigment problem is historical:
    Van Gogh's faded rosés (geranium lake) and many impressionist violets are documented
    losses; conservation science reads the survivors' chemistry backwards. Meanwhile the
    acrylic film's softness (glass transition near room temperature) attracts dirt it
    cannot shed and resists traditional solvent cleaning — an open conservation problem
    the 21st century bequeathed itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> New pigment chemistry still moves the field: YInMn
    blue (discovered 2009, commercialised 2016 — the first new inorganic blue in two
    centuries) added a genuinely new mixing behaviour to the chart. How should digital
    media's conservation (formats, screens, cloud dependency) join the permanence
    calculus? And can the pigment–binder–substrate system be modelled well enough to
    predict a work's century-scale appearance — the question where painting craft,
    conservation science and materials simulation now share one table? The AO2 strand's
    deepest point stands regardless: knowing the medium's properties is not technical
    trivia — it is the vocabulary in which expressive decisions are actually made.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mayer, <em>The Artist's Handbook of Materials and Techniques</em>; Eastaugh et al., <em>The Pigment Compendium</em>; ASTM D4302/D5067 lightfastness standards; the chemistry lesson's polymers chapter; the conservation lesson for permanence.</p>
